  abstract = {Inhibitory control is essential to daily function, and its disruptions are considered core elements of many psychiatric disorders. One common measure of inhibitory control is the congruency effect. However, despite its extensive use, recent research has reported low reliability of the congruency effect. Here, we asked whether it is possible to obtain precise individual estimates of the congruency effect. We provide a new resource dataset with extensive sampling of more than five thousand trials in nine participants across four inhibitory control tasks. With this dataset, we show that substantially higher numbers of trials are required for reliable individual results than are collected in many common tools (e.g., NIH Toolbox). Using two additional public datasets, we demonstrate that extensive sampling is necessary to reveal true individual differences in the congruency effect and improve observations from alternative modeling approaches. Intensive sampling provides an avenue for characterizing commonalities and differences in inhibitory control.},
